35/37 pass. Two failures — both `legacyCode`/`ghostField` against response side: my `validateDeclaredFields` (response side) returns `unverifiable` when the field isn't found in any 2xx schema, but semantically the correct answer is `not_found` — the schema is inspectable (it's a concrete object), the field simply doesn't exist. My `fieldStatusInSchema` returns 'not_found' for a missing property… wait, so why is it coming out 'unverifiable'?

`fieldStatusInSchema(tokenizeFieldPath('legacyCode'), quoteSchema)`: legacyCode → tokens [legacyCode]. current = quoteSchema (object with properties). properties[legacyCode] === undefined → should return not_found!

Oh wait — I got it. In validateDeclaredFields (response side), the loop is:
```js
for (const schema of schemas) {
  const { status } = fieldStatusInSchema(...);
  if (status === 'exists') return exists;
}
return { path, status: 'unverifiable' };
```
It returns 'unverifiable' if it's not found in any schema — but if at least one schema was inspectable, it should return 'not_found'. Distinction: 'unverifiable' should only be when no schema was inspectable at all (e.g., no 2xx response, or all type 'any'). Fix: track whether we saw a verifiable check:

```js
let sawVerifiable = false;
for (const schema of schemas) {
  const { status } = fieldStatusInSchema(...);
  if (status === 'exists') return { path, status };
  if (status === 'not_found') sawVerifiable = true;
}
return { path, status: sawVerifiable ? 'not_found' : 'unverifiable' };
```
With this, legacyCode → not_found ✓ (quoteSchema is verifiable).

